TODO: feature_hmp_deterministic_seal.py

Functional regtest scenario for deterministic seal weighting
(nHMPDeterministicSealHeight gate at src/chainparams.cpp, the anchor +
rebuild branch at the top of the HMP block in src/validation.cpp
ConnectBlock, and the raw-signer crediting in ConnectBlock,
RebuildHMPState, and RollforwardBlock).

The unit-level coverage already shipped in src/test/hmp_tests.cpp:

  DeterministicSeal_GateBoundary
      Confirms IsDeterministicSealActive(height) is off when the height
      is 0, on at and above a scheduled height, and independent of the
      nHMPSealAlgoFixHeight / nPrevSealHashFixHeight gates.

This functional scenario adds the end-to-end coverage the unit tests
cannot: that the SAME block is assigned the SAME nSealWeight (and so the
chain the SAME nChainSealWork) whether a node reached it by extending the
tip or by reorganizing onto it. That is the divergence the fix closes,
and it only manifests when seal weight is non-neutral, which needs more
than one privileged signer.

It is deferred because the regtest infrastructure to (a) stand up at
least two HMP identities that reach Stage 3 Elder status on a
controllable algo, (b) produce assembled seals with those signers, and
(c) drive a deterministic reorg across the activation height, is the same
multi-signer harness still missing for the prevSealHash scenario
(feature_hmp_prevseal_harmonization.py.TODO).

Scenario sketch (2 signers + 1 observer, all on regtest, gate enabled via
-testactivationheight=hmp_deterministic_seal@<N>):

  1. Start all nodes with the gate at height N, well inside Stage 4.
  2. Mine past N with both signers privileged, so blocks carry assembled
     seals whose weight depends on a privilege/commitment record sitting
     at a threshold boundary in the entry at tip - nHMPPrivilegeWindow.
  3. On the observer, record nSealWeight and nChainSealWork at some block
     B reached by straight extension.
  4. Invalidate a block below B on the observer, mine a competing same
     height block, then reconsider so the observer reorganizes back onto
     B (depth >= 1).
  5. Assert getsealstatus(B) and the block-index nChainSealWork at B are
     identical to step 3. Pre-fix they differ because the reorged
     observer weighs B against a window short by the reorg depth.
  6. Repeat with the gate set above the chain tip and assert the values
     DO differ, proving the gate, not an unrelated change, is responsible.
